# Target C-II — modern all-train gap forces a low-degree exceptional-face staircase

**Date:** 2026-09-15
**Status:** proof-only high-t structural strengthening
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II train-measure branch
**Dependencies:** scope-safe all-train gap `g=0.007707`; stopped-train/face ledger; Type-II force-hull perimeter convergence; hostile-audited isolate-host degree-7 theorem
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_low_degree_face_staircase.py`
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

Let

\[
C_{707}=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.007707,
\qquad \Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad t={q-k\over D}.
\]

For an integer `r>=5`, let

\[
F_{\le r}:=\sum_{d=5}^r f_d
\]

be the number of bounded exceptional contact faces of degree at most `r`.

For every fixed endpoint `t<=X`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {F_{\le r}\over D}
\ge
L_r(X):={ (r-3)C_{707}/2+\pi-(r+5)X/4\over r-4}.
}
\tag{LD.1}
\]

Thus `F_{<=r}=Omega(D)` whenever

\[
\boxed{
X<X_r:={2(r-3)C_{707}+4\pi\over r+5}.
}
\tag{LD.2}

The exact certificate gives

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
r & X_r\\ \hline
5 & >2.0215\\
6 & >2.1854\\
7 & >2.3220\\
10 & >2.6225\\
15 & >2.9230\\
22 & >3.1568.
\end{array}
\tag{LD.3}

## Proof

The scope-safe all-train theorem and exact stopped-train identity give

\[
{T-q/2\over D}
={t\over2}+2{f\over D}-{k\over2D}
\ge C_{707}-o(1).
\]

Since `k>=0`,

\[
\boxed{{f\over D}\ge {C_{707}\over2}-{t\over4}-o(1).}
\tag{LD.4}
\]

The exact outer ledger is `b+Q=2(q-k)`. The outer contact walk contains the force-hull boundary and `per(F)/D -> pi`, hence

\[
\boxed{{Q\over D}\le2t-\pi+o(1).}
\tag{LD.5}
\]

Now

\[
f=\sum_{d\ge5}f_d,
\qquad Q=\sum_{d\ge5}(d-4)f_d.
\]

Every face counted by `F_{<=r}` contributes at least one unit to `Q`; every remaining face has degree at least `r+1` and contributes at least `r-3`. Therefore

\[
Q\ge F_{\le r}+(r-3)(f-F_{\le r})
=(r-3)f-(r-4)F_{\le r},
\]

so

\[
\boxed{F_{\le r}\ge{(r-3)f-Q\over r-4}.}
\tag{LD.6}
\]

Substituting (LD.4)-(LD.5) proves (LD.1), and solving `L_r(X)>0` gives (LD.2). For `r=5`, this is exactly the modern pentagon inequality `P_5>=2f-Q`.

## Certified endpoint rows

The verifier proves

\[
\begin{array}{c|c|c}
X & r & F_{\le r}/D\text{ lower}\\ \hline
2.10 & 6 & >0.1175\\
2.18 & 6 & >0.0075\\
2.20 & 7 & >0.1220\\
2.30 & 7 & >0.0220\\
2.50 & 10 & >0.0766\\
3.00 & 22 & >0.0588.
\end{array}
\tag{LD.7}

Thus after the pentagon-only floor has vanished, the branch `t<=2.10` still contains more than `0.1175D-o(D)` bounded pentagon-or-hexagon faces.

## Source-empty 5/6 row

The hostile-audited isolate-host theorem is exact: a bounded canonical contact face containing an assigned contact-isolated source point has degree at least seven. Hence every face counted by `F_{<=6}` is source-empty.

Therefore throughout `t<=2.1854`, the forced low-degree population consists of source-empty convex equilateral unit-separated pentagons and hexagons. At `t<=2.10`, more than `0.1175D-o(D)` such empty chambers are present.

The existing Wegner/Groemer 5/6-face stability theorem can be applied facewise on this population without an interior-isolate correction. It still requires small face residual to conclude a hidden near-unit diagonal; no such residual smallness is asserted here.

## Terminal-train consequence

Every side occurrence of a bounded exceptional face is a terminal occurrence of its complete stopped train. A complete train has at most two terminal occurrences. Since every exceptional face has degree at least five,

\[
\boxed{N_{\le r}\ge {5\over2}F_{\le r},}
\tag{LD.8}

where `N_{<=r}` counts distinct stopped trains terminating on at least one degree-`<=r` exceptional face. At `t<=2.10`,

\[
\boxed{N_{\le6}>0.2937D-o(D).}
\tag{LD.9}

## Universal Type-II row

Consecutive loaded force-hull vertices are unit-separated, so `q<=per(F)`. Since `k>=0` and `per(F)/D->pi`, every Type-II sequence has

\[
\boxed{t\le\pi+o(1).}
\tag{LD.10}

The `r=22` threshold in (LD.3) is strictly above `pi`. Substituting the adverse endpoint directly gives the certified universal bounds

\[
\boxed{\liminf {F_{\le22}\over D}>0.0057,}
\tag{LD.11}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{N_{\le22}>0.0142D-o(D).}
\tag{LD.12}

Thus no Type-II branch can escape by sending essentially all bounded defect faces to unbounded degree.

This does not close C-II. The next useful steps are to control fixed-boundary residual on the linear 5/6 population, use the terminal-train population against phase/crossing capacity, or build a source-level local exchange on the source-empty low-degree chambers.

**Disposition:** proved a uniform low-degree exceptional-face staircase from the modern all-train gap. C-II and Level I remain open.
